  "query_preview": "..\nToday and Tonight: The Low is currently positioned over Central\nWashington and will continue to slide east through Monday. For \nMonday, precip chances will decrease through day as drier air \nfills in behind the the exiting system from west to east. Precip\nchances will be highest over the Idaho Panhandle and Northeast \nWashington. 24-hour 25-75 percent probabilities for Monday have \nthe Idaho Panhandle ranging from 0.3-1 inch. Extreme Eastern \nWashington, including Spokane and Pullman, ranges from 0-0.4 of \nan inch. The Central Basin and Cascades has only reaches a few \nhundredths. Winds will increase some Monday. West winds of 10-15\nmph with gusts of 15 to 25 mph will be possible, especially the\nUpper Columbia Basin, Palouse and the Spokane/CdA area. Highs \nMonday will be in the upper 50s and 60s over the east third of \nWA and ID, while central WA will see upper 60s and 70s.\n\nTuesday through Thursday: The ensembles are in decent agreement\nfor the extended. A flat ridge pattern will set up over the \nregion as the Low exits. It will lead to dry, warming trend \nthrough the end of the week. Winds will be main concern \nWednesday and Thursday. For Wednesday, South to North winds will\nbring breezy conditions across the Waterville Plateau and \nOkanogan Valley. Sustained winds will be in the teens with gusts\nup to 25 mph. For Thursday, the winds spread across the Basin \nwith sustained in the teens with gusts up to 30 mph. Locally \nstronger winds are possible over the Waterville Plateau with \ngusts up to 40 mph. Blowing dust could be a concern in the area.\nPrecip chances will be less than 10 percent through Thursday. \nHighs will be in the 70s for Tuesday and climb into the upper \n70s and 80s for Wednesday and Thursday. Overnight lows will be \nin the 40s to low 50s.\n\nFriday and Saturday: Another trough will drop in from BC Friday\nas remnants of tropical system begin to press moisture into the\nPacific Northwest. It is leading to increasing precip chances \ngoing into the weekend. NBM thunderstorm potential in around \n10-20 percent across the Inland Northwest with highest percents \nover Northeast WA and Idaho Panhandle late Friday and Saturday. \nIt is too early to have much confidence. Highs will trend cooler\nfor the weekend with upper 60s and 70s expected. Lows expected \nto be in the upper 30s and 40s. /JDC",
